As of FY 2025–26 (AY 2026–27), the **NPS (National Pension System)** is regulated by PFRDA under the PFRDA Act, 2013 and is open to all Indian citizens aged 18–70. PFRDA defines NPS as a defined-contribution pension system where your retirement corpus is determined by contributions made, investment returns earned, and years invested — not a pre-set pension amount. This NPS calculator models all three variables so you can see exactly what drives your retirement number before you commit.

**How the NPS corpus formula works:** The calculator applies the standard future value of annuity formula — FV = P × [((1 + r)^n − 1) / r] — where P is your monthly contribution, r is the monthly return rate (annual rate ÷ 12), and n is the total number of months invested. The resulting corpus is then split at exit: one portion goes to a lump sum withdrawal, and the remainder purchases an annuity that pays monthly pension for life.

**The December 2025 rule change you need to know:** PFRDA notified the NPS (Exits and Withdrawals) Amendment Regulations, 2025 in December 2025, significantly changing exit rules for non-government subscribers. The mandatory annuity purchase has been cut from 40% to 20% of the corpus. If your corpus at retirement is ₹8 lakh or less, you can withdraw 100% as a lump sum with no annuity requirement at all. If your corpus is above ₹12 lakh, the new 80:20 split applies — 80% lump sum, 20% to annuity. Government employees retain the earlier 60:40 rule. This calculator defaults to the 60:40 structure; adjust the annuity percentage slider to model the new 80:20 scenario if you are a non-government subscriber.

**Who should actually use NPS?** Three situations where NPS makes clear sense: (1) You are under 40, in the old tax regime, and want to claim the additional ₹50,000 deduction under Section 80CCD(1B) — this deduction is not available in the new tax regime, so it only helps old-regime taxpayers. (2) Your employer offers NPS contributions under Section 80CCD(2) — employer NPS contributions up to 14% of basic salary remain deductible even in the new tax regime, making employer-NPS a near-automatic win. (3) You want market-linked exposure (up to 75% equity allocation) in a pension wrapper with a structured retirement payout.

**NPS vs EPF vs PPF — the honest comparison:** EPF gives a guaranteed 8.25% return (FY 2023–24 rate) with full liquidity post-retirement. PPF gives a tax-free guaranteed 7.1% (as of Q1 FY 2025–26) with a 15-year lock-in. NPS offers market-linked returns that have historically ranged from 10–13% annually for equity-heavy Tier I funds, but 20–40% of your corpus must fund a 5–7% annuity — that annuity portion earns less than what EPF or PPF would. Use the [PPF Calculator](/india/ppf-calculator/) and [EPF Calculator](/india/epf-calculator/) alongside this NPS calculator to model your full retirement picture.

**Tier I vs Tier II — in two sentences:** NPS Tier I is the mandatory, tax-advantaged retirement account with withdrawal restrictions until age 60. NPS Tier II is a voluntary savings account with no withdrawal restrictions, no tax deduction on contributions (except for government employees), and no mandatory annuity — it functions more like a mutual fund than a pension account.

**Auto Choice vs Active Choice:** Auto Choice (Lifecycle Funds) automatically reduces equity allocation as you age — the Aggressive Life Cycle Fund (LC75) starts at 75% equity and steps down after age 35. Active Choice lets you manually set equity up to 75%, corporate bonds up to 100%, and government securities up to 100%. Investors under 40 with a higher risk tolerance typically benefit from Active Choice or LC75 for the equity growth potential.

Use this NPS calculator to compare scenarios: different contribution levels, earlier vs later start ages, and the impact of higher equity allocation on your projected retirement corpus.

